# rustkern/dosmem.rs - #745: XMS 3.0 (extended memory) and LIM EMS 4.0 (expanded
# memory) for the 16-bit DOS guest. These are the reason Disney's Aladdin gets
# past "XMS allocation error..": the wall was memory managers, not opcodes.
#
# NOTE FOR ANYONE TEMPTED TO DELETE ONE. Neither manager is reached through an
# obvious call site. XMS is reached by a FAR CALL to an address handed out by
# INT 2Fh AX=4310h, through the interpreter's far-call trap; EMS is reached only
# after the guest OPENS "EMMXXXX0" as a file and IOCTLs the handle. So a
# grep for callers under-counts both, and the dispatchers look dead. They are
# not: dos/dosexec.c holds the only C references.
# rustkern/dosmcb.rs - #172: what INT 21h AH=48h reports as the largest
# free block, and AH=26h Create New PSP. The free-space report used to be
# 0xA000 - bump_pointer, which answers 2 paragraphs once a guest has done
# the standard allocate-high dance, whatever is actually free below.
# Stunts' LOAD.EXE asks exactly that question and exits on the answer.
dos_mcb_largest_free_rs
dos_psp_create_rs
dos_mcb_selftest_rs

